[{:db/id 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b11f04e7-08c8-5f47-98ca-f9bed3019d8e" "question-e01a2c34-8ac1-5c1d-8c31-0ae504dfb7c8" "question-4d60fb24-9c3e-583a-bd44-72b00cae8e44" "question-f2ec4fda-aa3e-5894-ae68-873ea21a07ad" "question-83a10579-467e-5020-9446-f0bcbebf0762"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142589 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $4 \\times 1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7},$ expressing the result as a mixed number." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 7) + 2}{7} = \\dfrac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{7} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{36}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{7} = 5\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 5\\,\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8cf2b8e2-2314-5e9d-b171-6805247e3cfc" "question-b13fbae9-cf7a-599c-b37d-114ee2f7bfd9" "question-9d97e614-0772-528b-a353-38b67fdb040e" "question-b0e3e224-39c7-55c1-9017-33330a41adbe" "question-d47e615f-58e6-5902-88f8-098ef1f1c1bf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142654 :question/problem "Multiply $4 \\times 2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{36}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = \\dfrac{36 \\div 4}{4 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{9}{1} = 9\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f78e4f9-faf2-56cf-8be7-d24c50f82032" "question-9c5c53ba-1c75-5a47-95b1-d73845f940f1" "question-bae0062b-f5ac-5baa-b921-966c2cbaf487" "question-545b365b-c759-5099-8acb-b948f190db31"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186142653 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9} \\times 3,$ expressing the result as a mixed number." :question/worked-solution "First, we write  $2\\,\\dfrac{2}{9}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 9)+2}{9}= \\dfrac{20}{9}\n$$\n\nWe also  write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}9} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}9$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{3}{1} & =\\frac{20}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{9} \\\\  & =20\\times \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b11f04e7-08c8-5f47-98ca-f9bed3019d8e" :question/id #uuid "b11f04e7-08c8-5f47-98ca-f9bed3019d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152210" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\\times 4=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{5}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{1}=\\frac{12\\times 4}{5\\times 1}=\\frac{48}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $48.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c47f8ec3-ee07-5374-a258-a7ce3473e0fd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c47f8ec3-ee07-5374-a258-a7ce3473e0fd" :answer-field/id #uuid "c47f8ec3-ee07-5374-a258-a7ce3473e0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-83d6cf82-ccb6-5ce3-8b1f-5da5f5e4dd28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f6c6ac5-f257-5208-a9b6-bf5684ce5d65" "answer-bd9e3690-7acc-52fb-a6ee-a9d34c24eedf" "answer-b0e7e052-5ff9-5234-9b35-c7ab602fdaab" "answer-83d6cf82-ccb6-5ce3-8b1f-5da5f5e4dd28" "answer-651fa64e-24e5-5050-97cf-defab8eed92a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f6c6ac5-f257-5208-a9b6-bf5684ce5d65" :answer/id #uuid "8f6c6ac5-f257-5208-a9b6-bf5684ce5d65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd9e3690-7acc-52fb-a6ee-a9d34c24eedf" :answer/id #uuid "bd9e3690-7acc-52fb-a6ee-a9d34c24eedf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0e7e052-5ff9-5234-9b35-c7ab602fdaab" :answer/id #uuid "b0e7e052-5ff9-5234-9b35-c7ab602fdaab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83d6cf82-ccb6-5ce3-8b1f-5da5f5e4dd28" :answer/id #uuid "83d6cf82-ccb6-5ce3-8b1f-5da5f5e4dd28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-651fa64e-24e5-5050-97cf-defab8eed92a" :answer/id #uuid "651fa64e-24e5-5050-97cf-defab8eed92a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e01a2c34-8ac1-5c1d-8c31-0ae504dfb7c8" :question/id #uuid "e01a2c34-8ac1-5c1d-8c31-0ae504dfb7c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-57086" :question/problem "$4\\times 2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 12}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{48}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{5}=9\\,\\text{R}3=9\\,\\frac{3}{5}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6109e18b-2bfc-5e78-b42e-1e8202e29023"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6109e18b-2bfc-5e78-b42e-1e8202e29023" :answer-field/id #uuid "6109e18b-2bfc-5e78-b42e-1e8202e29023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eb5c2727-e63e-59b7-9ce6-71a9f7a9cb31" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7584e33-8518-5779-a7c0-78c9ce92644a" "answer-59e8a5f1-17cd-53a8-bdf4-67081f7231ed" "answer-cf14af03-26e6-5f14-97c4-632688784f4e" "answer-758054c1-1ef3-5ca4-a9c2-83a16ff7f154" "answer-eb5c2727-e63e-59b7-9ce6-71a9f7a9cb31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7584e33-8518-5779-a7c0-78c9ce92644a" :answer/id #uuid "c7584e33-8518-5779-a7c0-78c9ce92644a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59e8a5f1-17cd-53a8-bdf4-67081f7231ed" :answer/id #uuid "59e8a5f1-17cd-53a8-bdf4-67081f7231ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf14af03-26e6-5f14-97c4-632688784f4e" :answer/id #uuid "cf14af03-26e6-5f14-97c4-632688784f4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-758054c1-1ef3-5ca4-a9c2-83a16ff7f154" :answer/id #uuid "758054c1-1ef3-5ca4-a9c2-83a16ff7f154" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb5c2727-e63e-59b7-9ce6-71a9f7a9cb31" :answer/id #uuid "eb5c2727-e63e-59b7-9ce6-71a9f7a9cb31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d60fb24-9c3e-583a-bd44-72b00cae8e44" :question/id #uuid "4d60fb24-9c3e-583a-bd44-72b00cae8e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-57052" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{7}\\times 6=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{7}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{3}{7}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{7}=\\frac{(1\\times 7)+3}{7}=\\frac{10}{7}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{7}\\times \\frac{6}{1}=\\frac{10\\times 6}{7\\times 1}=\\frac{60}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $60.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-92b5cb31-42a5-58be-9a5b-ca75a546decd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-92b5cb31-42a5-58be-9a5b-ca75a546decd" :answer-field/id #uuid "92b5cb31-42a5-58be-9a5b-ca75a546decd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6f4afde-723d-5479-b038-a2980e56f1be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-633d3d2b-b960-5df4-8abc-904c3a72bbc8" "answer-64d583fc-0a22-540d-91f5-7806df5fab9f" "answer-35846e5e-d6aa-5b07-9366-d3c61775b442" "answer-a8f82b8f-b607-5e9b-b4fd-379545b9b00e" "answer-d6f4afde-723d-5479-b038-a2980e56f1be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-633d3d2b-b960-5df4-8abc-9